.TH SH-TILES 1
.SH NAME
sh-tiles \- build a screen of tiles from a shell script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load tiles
.PP
.B tiles new
.I WxH
[
.I scale
] [
.I title
]
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.IR command ...
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B draw
.I surface
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B write
.I surface file
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B bind
.I action key ...
.br
.BI ${tiles " id " wait}
[
.I ms
]
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B events
.I name
[
.I ms
]
.br
.BI ${tiles " id " keys}
|
.BI ${tiles " id " pressed}
|
.BI ${tiles " id " ms}
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B pace
.I ms
.br
.B tiles del
.I id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sh-tiles
gives shell scripts
.IR tiles (2)'s
own command language, the way
.IR sh-plot (1)
gives them
.IR plot (2)'s
and
.IR sh-tk (1)
gives them
.IR tk (2)'s.
.PP
What that is for:
.IR tiles (2)
draws from named palettes, named sheets of art and named surfaces, all
of them made by text, and its art and its levels are ordinary files.
Put those two together and a screenful of 8-bit graphics is something a
shell script can build \- which is the same trick that makes a Tk window
something a shell script can build, and for the same reason.
.PP
Nothing in this module knows what any of the commands mean.
Everything after the handle is forwarded verbatim to
.IR tiles (2)'s
.IR cmd ,
and only
.BR new ,
.BR del ,
.B draw
and
.B write
\- the window and the file, which
.IR tiles (2)
has no opinion about \- are special-cased first words.
A forwarded command's own failure is reported as a soft
.BR $status ,
which is
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
convention for a bad raw tk command; only handle and usage errors raise.
.PP
In
.B ${}
the answer is the value, so a script can ask a question:
.B ${tiles $id size level}
is
.BR "256 96" ,
and a command that fails hands back its
.B !
message rather than aborting the script.
.SS Reading, and keeping time
A script that can draw and cannot read has made a picture.
.B Bind
names an action and the keys that work it \-
.B "tiles $id bind fire Space x"
\- and nothing here knows what
.B fire
means, which is the same trade
.IR tiles (2)'s
own commands make.
Keys are single characters, or
.BR Left ,
.BR Right ,
.BR Up ,
.BR Down ,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR Pgup ,
.BR Pgdown ,
.BR Space ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR Enter ,
.BR Esc .
.PP
.B Keys
answers the actions held right now and
.B pressed
those newly pressed since the last ask;
both drain what the window has delivered and return at once, so a loop
asks once a frame and carries on.
.B Ms
is the clock and
.B pace
sleeps until a given number of milliseconds since the last
.BR pace ,
against an absolute deadline rather than for a fixed time \- which is
why
.IR frameclock (2)
exists for Limbo and is needed here for the same reason.
.SS Waiting
A poll is not a wait, and a script that polls has to have a frame to
hang the poll on.
There are two ways here and they are for different things.
.PP
.BI ${tiles " id " wait\ ms}
blocks until a key arrives or until
.I ms
have passed, whichever comes first, and answers
.B tick
or
.BR "key " \fIaction\fP...
That is an
.BR alt ,
done in Limbo because the shell has none of its own, and it is what a
game loop wants: one call, no plumbing, and no frame of latency on a
key.
With no
.I ms
it waits for a key and nothing else.
.PP
.BI "tiles " id " events " name \ ms
is the other answer and the more Inferno one: the same stream as a
.I file
in
.BR /chan ,
whose read blocks.
Waiting is what a read does, so nothing new has to be invented \- and
because it is a file, another process can wait on it, a script can
filter it through
.IR sed (1),
and exporting the namespace exports the game's input.
.IP
.EX
tiles $id events warren.ev 110
ev = `{read < /chan/warren.ev}
.EE
.PP
What that costs is a process and the keyboard: once the server has it,
.B wait
and
.B keys
see nothing and say so.
.PP
The shell's own answer to this question is
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
.B ${recv}
and
.BR ${alt} ,
which are the right thing and are not reachable from here:
that module's channel table is private to it, the same way its window
table is.
A file is reachable from anywhere, which is the argument for the second
form.
.PP
Ask for these with
.B ${}
and not with
.BR `{} .
A backquote runs its block in a subprocess, and a subprocess reaching
into the parent's Tk window deadlocks; the
.B ${}
form answers in this process.
.PP
.B Draw
puts a named surface in the window;
.B write
puts it in a file, as a line of
.B "W H BYTESPERPIXEL"
followed by the pixels, so a script can build a screen and keep it with
nobody looking at a window.
.SH EXAMPLE
A whole screen, with the art and the level in files and not one line of
Limbo:
.IP
.EX
load std
load tiles
id = ${tiles new 256x96 2 \(aqtiles from sh\(aq}
tiles $id palette turf  dark.black dark.green  mid.lime   light.lime
tiles $id palette brick dark.black dark.orange mid.orange light.orange
tiles $id palette hud   dark.black pale.grey   pale.grey  pale.grey
tiles $id sheet art /lib/tiles/demo.chr
tiles $id font font
tiles $id surface level 256 96 dark.black
tiles $id map level art /lib/tiles/demo.map
tiles $id text level 8 4 hud BUILT BY SH
tiles $id draw level
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/tiles.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
