.SH DIAGNOSTICS
What this costs, measured with
.B ms
from a script, on this host at 256 by 96 and scale 3:
.IP
.EX
an empty shell loop            18 us an iteration
a put command                  26 us
a whole-surface blit           14 us
a draw to the window          150 us
a complete game frame         220 us
.EE
.PP
That frame is
.IR warren (1)'s,
which has nothing moving but the player.
.IR Leap (1)
scrolls a level twice the width of the window and walks walkers, and
costs 2500 microseconds a frame with none of them and about a
millisecond more for each one \- so four is comfortable at 60Hz, twelve
is the edge of it, and forty is twenty-three frames a second.
The cost is round trips and not drawing: a walker is eight or nine
substitutions at about 26 microseconds each and one
.BR put .
.PP
A frame there is reading the keys, asking the level what is in the way,
blitting the level, drawing the player, drawing the score and putting it
on the screen.
At 220 microseconds that is 1.3 per cent of a 60Hz budget, so the answer
to whether a game can be written in the shell is not "nearly".
.SH BUGS
.B del
forgets the handle and leaves the window on screen, because
.I tkclient->wmctl
with
.B exit
would exit the whole process group and this module is a guest in
whatever process runs the script.
.IR Sh-plot (1)
and
.IR sh-tk (1)
make the same trade for the same reason.
.PP
There is no way to hand a surface back to a Limbo program from here, so
a script can build a screen and draw it or write it, and cannot pass it
to something else.
.PP
Only one reader waits on an event file at a time; a second is told so
rather than silently replacing the first.
The queue behind it is bounded at 32 events, so a script that stops
reading loses events rather than making the server eat memory.
.PP
.B Wait
answers on a press and not on a release, because a loop woken by
somebody letting go of a key has nothing to do.  A script that wants to
see releases has to poll
.B keys
as well.
